Negative control for the guest entry answering to what the session store can
actually do (`R7-13`).

Drops the two session clauses, which is the pre-ruling shape: the pool offers
"Continue as guest" beside a live client or staff session. Nothing throws and
the control still pushes `/as/guest` — the url moves, the page refuses the
scope — but the store's write gate re-resolves the active pointer over the live
cookies and promotes that same session straight back, so the identity never
becomes guest and the pool's own trigger keeps drawing the client. That is the
control that renders and does nothing.

Only the availability law is on trial: guest is still gated on the app's
`allowedScopes` and still hidden once the url already names the guest scope, so
the control cannot go red by offering guest everywhere.

Apply from the repo root: `git apply <this file>`; revert with `git apply -R`.

Reds: the pool's guest cases — with a client (or staff) session seeded the
guest entry must not render, and with no session at all it must render and
carry the page to `/as/guest`.

  FOR THE PROVER: authored by the developer seat, which knew the line it wrote;
  the patch is a source mutation and grades nothing. It was APPLY-CHECKED only
  (applies cleanly, reverts cleanly, `git status` returns to its prior state) —
  the spec it must red did not exist at authoring time, so there is no author
  RED run to quote and none should be inferred. Apply it blind, confirm the
  guest cases go red, then revert.

